From 4e7a832f6706a3aaa7548a87513effbde0047815 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 22:45:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(gateway): salt a relayed Messages reply's tool_use id the
 client has seen again (#4910)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Claude Code keeps every tool_use id of a session in one Set and drops a
block whose id it has already seen; a turn whose only block is dropped
becomes "[Tool use interrupted]", and the agent loops on calls that did
run (#4910). An upstream that speaks Messages itself and is relayed as it
came may number its calls' ids by their place in the reply (LiteLLM's
bridge: fc_0 on every turn), so the id one turn went out under comes again
on the next, and every later call of the session is dropped.

A relayed reply's tool_use id the client already holds — an assistant
block of the request the reply answers, or one the reply itself said
before — now goes out under the mark, the upstream's own id and a fresh
salt, which nothing the client holds names; an id the client has seen no
more goes as it came, so an upstream whose ids are its own is relayed
byte for byte. The answer comes back under the salted id and goes to the
upstream as the id it gave, on the call and on the result answering it,
so a strict upstream keeps its own pairing and its prompt cache.

A reply translated from another wire needed nothing: the model's own
call ids pass through there, one fresh a reply.

Run: TestMessagesRelayedToolUseIDsUniqueAcrossTurns fails on the old
code with the one fc_0 on both turns and passes with the change;
TestMessagesRelayedToolUseIDsUniqueInATurn,
TestMessagesRelayedOwnToolUseIDsUntouched,
TestMessagesTranslatedToolUseIDsUniqueAcrossTurns green.

Generated-by: GLM-5.3-Flash (ZCode)
